// C11 6.9p3/p5: one body per function per TU. C has no overloading, so a
// second body for a C-linkage name is a redefinition whatever its spelling —
// `int f(void) {...}` twice reached MIR as two definitions and died there
// ("MIR fatal error: Repeated item declaration f", no location, process exit).
// Now refused beside parseFunction's C-linkage signature-clash check, from
// FuncDef::body_parsed. testredeclfuncokc is the allowed twin; the C++-linkage
// same-signature case stays fold_same_signature_overload's (type-model twins).
// Oracle: gcc and clang "redefinition of 'f'" (g++ / clang++ likewise).
#include <stdio.h>
int f(void) { return 1; }
int f(void) { return 2; }
int main(void)
{
	printf("%d\n", f());
	return 0;
}
